---
import Content from "../Content.astro";
import SectionHead from "../Section.astro";
import ProjectPubs from "../../layouts/ProjectPubs.astro";
import PubButton from "../buttons/PubButton.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import horseRangeChart from "../../assets/img/horse-range-chart.png";
import museumLogos from "../../assets/img/museum-logos.png";
import cntl from "cntl";

const firstParagraphClasses = cntl`
  first-letter:text-8xl
  first-letter:font-semibold
  first-letter:mr-2
  first-letter:float-left
`;

const spaceClasses = cntl`mr-1`;
---

<div>
  <SectionHead
    title="Fossil Horse Macroevolution"
    subtitle="Investigating macroevolutionary transitions in Neogene Equidae using phenomics and machine learning"
  />
  <Content>
    <p class={firstParagraphClasses}>
      Fossil horses (Family Equidae) provide one of the most complete records of
      mammalian evolution in the fossil record, spanning over 55 million years
      in North America. My dissertation research focuses on macroevolutionary
      transitions in Neogene fossil horses, leveraging phenomics — the
      large-scale, quantitative measurement of phenotypic variation, alongside
      machine learning to detect patterns of evolutionary change across geographic 
      and temporal scales. By integrating museum collections from across North America
      with computational approaches, I aim to reveal how populations of fossil horses
      changed morphologically across major climatic and environmental
      transitions.
       <Content>
    <p class={firstParagraphClasses}>
   <figure class="float-right ml-4 mb-2 w-[400px]">
  <Image
    src={horseRangeChart}
    alt="Geological range and North American Land Mammal Ages of latest Hemphillian equid faunas"
    width={400}
    class="rounded-xl"
  />
  <figcaption class="text-xs text-porsche-950 dark:text-porsche-50 mt-2 pl-3 border-l-2 border-porsche-950 dark:border-porsche-50 leading-relaxed">
    <span class="font-semibold">Figure 2.</span>
    Geological ranges and North American Land Mammal Ages (NALMAs) of latest Hemphillian (Hh4) equid faunas in North America, including four widespread and two endemic species (Killingsworth & MacFadden, 2023). <span class="italic">(Killingsworth and MacFadden, 2023)</span>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      A central component of this work involves applying interpretable deep
      learning models to images of fossil horse teeth, which preserve detailed
      morphological information about diet, ecology, and phylogenetic
      relationships. This approach allows me to quantify subtle variation across
      large sample sizes that would be impractical to measure manually, opening
      new windows into paleopopulation structure and evolutionary dynamics. This 
      research was selected for presentation in the Romer Prize Session at the 2026 
      Society of Vertebrate Paleontology annual meeting.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      I also investigate species occurrence patterns among Late Miocene horses in Florida, examining whether gaps in the fossil record reflect genuine ecological absences, sampling biases, or both. This work addresses a fundamental challenge in interpreting evolutionary and biogeographic patterns from museum collections.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <SectionHead
    title="Publications"
  <div class="mt-8 mb-2 text-semibold text-lg">Publications:</div>
